WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.560
Sweden's 2016 nuclear capacity tax aimed to favor renewables but triggered early reactor

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:14.680
closures and higher coal imports. This is unintended consequences, episode 127. Swedish policy

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:20.420
makers wanted an electricity system that could grow rapidly with wind and solar while keeping

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.780
carbon emissions low. They viewed the tax as a neutral fiscal tool that would reflect

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:31.880
the full system costs of large thermal plants and generate revenue that could support grid upgrades.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.640
At the time, nuclear units still supplied roughly 40% of Swedish electricity,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.420
and their long remaining technical life made them appear as potential obstacles to faster renewable

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.460
build-out. The decision drew on standard economic reasoning that a capacity charge would encourage

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.660
operators to retire plants once marginal costs exceeded those of newer technologies.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:00.840
Lawmakers therefore treated the measure as a modest correction rather than a direct phase-out order.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.720
They reasoned that the revenue could fund transmission lines and balancing resources needed for variable renewables,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:14.440
and they expected existing nuclear operators to continue running units whose variable costs stayed competitive.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:22.960
The approach aligned with prevailing models that treated capacity payments as technology neutral signals rather than retirement mandates.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.400
rates. The tax took effect in 2016 as an annual charge based on installed capacity, rather

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.680
than actual generation. Proponents in the governing coalition argued it would raise several

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.960
hundred million crore per year, while remaining technology neutral on paper. Reactor owners

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.660
immediately signaled that the added fixed cost would shorten the economic life of units

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.980
already facing maintenance decisions. Some analysts warned that the tax ignored the

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.080
low marginal operating cost of nuclear, and the value of its dispatchable, carbon-free

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.580
output during periods of low wind or hydro. The first retirement announcements followed within

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:07.200
two years. Operators calculated that the fixed levy, when spread across remaining operating

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.260
hours, raised the effective cost per megawatt hour enough to tip marginal units into negative net

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:19.520
present value well before their technical end of life. Because the tax applied regardless of output,

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:27.640
It could not be avoided by increasing generation during high-price hours, which removed one traditional lever for recovering fixed charges.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:34.960
Because the tax raised fixed costs without changing the near-zero marginal cost of running a reactor once it was online,

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:41.680
operators chose to close units whose fuel and maintenance expenses remained below the cost of new renewables.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.840
Four reactors were taken offline ahead of their original schedules.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.140
In years when reservoir levels were low, Swedish imports rose, and a measurable share of

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.600
that power came from coal plants in neighboring Denmark and Poland.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.980
Grid data showed increased import dependence precisely during the hours when the retired

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.400
nuclear capacity would have been most valuable.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.920
The early closures therefore shifted the timing of decarbonization rather than accelerating

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:13.640
it, because the replacement power carried higher emissions than the reactors it displaced.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.140
A secondary effect appeared in neighboring markets.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.760
Danish and Polish coal units gained additional running hours, extending the economic life

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.340
of assets that might otherwise have retired sooner.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:31.480
The policy also altered investment signals for the remaining Swedish nuclear fleet, prompting

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.140
further reviews of long-term operation decisions.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.700
When hydro-output dropped, the missing base load forced the system to draw on the marginal

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:45.060
resource in the Nordic Baltic Polish interconnection, which at those moments was often coal.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.260
The arithmetic was straightforward.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:53.920
Each closed reactor removed roughly 500 to 1000 megawatts of always available, zero emission

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:59.800
generation, whose operating costs sat below the variable cost of coal units, yet the tax

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.460
had already been paid whether the reactor ran or not.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.020
This mismatch between the policies fixed cost focus and the systems need for low carbon

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:14.140
energy during scarcity hours, produced the import surge. By the early 2020s, the tax had

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:19.080
been repealed after the scale of the import shift became clear to grid operators and energy

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:24.480
agencies. Replacement capacity planning shifted toward a broader mix that again included

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.400
existing nuclear. No new reactors were ordered as a direct result, yet the episode prompted

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.820
renewed discussion of how capacity charges interact with low carbon base load.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:40.520
The episode left Sweden with a slightly higher reliance on cross-border flows during dry

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.220
years than would have existed without the tax.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.860
Subsequent policy reviews examined whether similar fixed charges should include exemptions

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:53.220
or credits for carbon-free output to avoid repeating the timing mismatch.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:59.260
Incentive structures that target one margin can shift retirement decisions even when marginal

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:01.880
costs remain favorable to the desired outcome.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:06.980
complex energy systems transmit policy changes across borders and across

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.660
weather dependent resources, so second order effects often appear in neighboring

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.740
markets or backup fuels. When designing instruments to steer technology transitions

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.440
it is useful to test whether the tax or subsidy changes the actual operating

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.220
decision that matters most. How might similar capacity charges now being

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.880
considered in other countries interact with the remaining low carbon fleets? We'll

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.400
leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking through this one with me. If you know someone

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.720
who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you tomorrow with

